# Lab 07: classification & routing against Ollama (llama3.2).
# One classify request, shown on three inlined inquiries: a routine conditions
# question (inq-0005), an emergency (inq-0041), and the deliberately ambiguous
# two-queue message (inq-0035). The "format" field is a JSON schema with the
# category enum, so the model must answer with one of the seven labels and
# nothing else. To classify the rest of inquiries-slice.jsonl, copy request 1
# and swap the message text after "Message:".

### 1. Routine conditions question, inq-0005 (expect {"category": "conditions"})
POST http://localhost:11434/api/chat
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "model": "llama3.2",
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "You are the triage system for the Trailhead Guides shared inbox. Classify the visitor message into exactly one category.\n\n- permit: reserving, changing, canceling, or paying for a permit, pass, or reservation, including billing problems and missing confirmations for a permit application.\n- conditions: asking whether a trail, road, or area is open, safe, or passable right now: snow, water levels, washouts, wildlife activity, closures.\n- complaint: unhappy about a park facility, service, or staff member and wants it acknowledged or fixed.\n- lost-and-found: reporting a lost or found physical item.\n- emergency: a person may be hurt, missing, or in danger right now and needs immediate human attention.\n- general: anything else: park rules, fees, trip planning, questions that fit none of the above.\n- unsure: two different queues both have to act before this message can be resolved, so no single queue owns it. The case that qualifies: the sender asks about trail conditions AND asks someone to change, refund, or cancel a booking. Trail info cannot issue a refund, and the permits office does not decide whether a trail is passable, so a human reads this queue and splits the work. Also use unsure when the message fits none of the categories above.\n\nDecide in this order. First, if anyone might be hurt, missing, or in danger, answer emergency and stop; never answer unsure for those, even when the message also mentions permits, conditions, or a lost item. Second, if one queue can resolve the whole message on its own, answer that queue; a booking or reservation problem with nothing else attached is permit, not unsure. Third, only if two queues must both act, answer unsure. Unsure is not a catch-all for anything hard.\n\nMessage:\nGood morning, we heard from another hiker that the bridge on the Avalanche Lake Trail washed out last week. Is that true? We have a family trip planned for June 27 and my mother uses trekking poles, she cannot ford a creek. Is there an alternate route to the lake or should we pick a different hike?"
    }
  ],
  "format": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "category": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": ["permit", "conditions", "complaint", "lost-and-found", "emergency", "general", "unsure"]
      }
    },
    "required": ["category"]
  },
  "stream": false
}

### 2. The one that must never be missed, inq-0041 (expect {"category": "emergency"})
POST http://localhost:11434/api/chat
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "model": "llama3.2",
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "You are the triage system for the Trailhead Guides shared inbox. Classify the visitor message into exactly one category.\n\n- permit: reserving, changing, canceling, or paying for a permit, pass, or reservation, including billing problems and missing confirmations for a permit application.\n- conditions: asking whether a trail, road, or area is open, safe, or passable right now: snow, water levels, washouts, wildlife activity, closures.\n- complaint: unhappy about a park facility, service, or staff member and wants it acknowledged or fixed.\n- lost-and-found: reporting a lost or found physical item.\n- emergency: a person may be hurt, missing, or in danger right now and needs immediate human attention.\n- general: anything else: park rules, fees, trip planning, questions that fit none of the above.\n- unsure: two different queues both have to act before this message can be resolved, so no single queue owns it. The case that qualifies: the sender asks about trail conditions AND asks someone to change, refund, or cancel a booking. Trail info cannot issue a refund, and the permits office does not decide whether a trail is passable, so a human reads this queue and splits the work. Also use unsure when the message fits none of the categories above.\n\nDecide in this order. First, if anyone might be hurt, missing, or in danger, answer emergency and stop; never answer unsure for those, even when the message also mentions permits, conditions, or a lost item. Second, if one queue can resolve the whole message on its own, answer that queue; a booking or reservation problem with nothing else attached is permit, not unsure. Third, only if two queues must both act, answer unsure. Unsure is not a catch-all for anything hard.\n\nMessage:\nMy dad slipped on scree on the Beehive descent, we're just past the ladder section. His ankle is swollen bad, can't put weight on it. We have water and I have 2 bars of signal. 2 adults 1 teen. How do we get help up here? Submitting this because 911 kept dropping."
    }
  ],
  "format": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "category": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": ["permit", "conditions", "complaint", "lost-and-found", "emergency", "general", "unsure"]
      }
    },
    "required": ["category"]
  },
  "stream": false
}

### 3. The deliberately ambiguous one, inq-0035 (expect {"category": "unsure"}: permits and trail info both have to act)
POST http://localhost:11434/api/chat
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "model": "llama3.2",
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "You are the triage system for the Trailhead Guides shared inbox. Classify the visitor message into exactly one category.\n\n- permit: reserving, changing, canceling, or paying for a permit, pass, or reservation, including billing problems and missing confirmations for a permit application.\n- conditions: asking whether a trail, road, or area is open, safe, or passable right now: snow, water levels, washouts, wildlife activity, closures.\n- complaint: unhappy about a park facility, service, or staff member and wants it acknowledged or fixed.\n- lost-and-found: reporting a lost or found physical item.\n- emergency: a person may be hurt, missing, or in danger right now and needs immediate human attention.\n- general: anything else: park rules, fees, trip planning, questions that fit none of the above.\n- unsure: two different queues both have to act before this message can be resolved, so no single queue owns it. The case that qualifies: the sender asks about trail conditions AND asks someone to change, refund, or cancel a booking. Trail info cannot issue a refund, and the permits office does not decide whether a trail is passable, so a human reads this queue and splits the work. Also use unsure when the message fits none of the categories above.\n\nDecide in this order. First, if anyone might be hurt, missing, or in danger, answer emergency and stop; never answer unsure for those, even when the message also mentions permits, conditions, or a lost item. Second, if one queue can resolve the whole message on its own, answer that queue; a booking or reservation problem with nothing else attached is permit, not unsure. Third, only if two queues must both act, answer unsure. Unsure is not a catch-all for anything hard.\n\nMessage:\nHi, I have a backcountry permit that includes a night at the Avalanche Lake area on June 24 (conf #GL-2026-07733). With the bridge out, is my itinerary even doable, and if not, will you let me swap that night for a different site without penalty, or refund it? I need to know before we leave Thursday. Thanks, Priya"
    }
  ],
  "format": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "category": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": ["permit", "conditions", "complaint", "lost-and-found", "emergency", "general", "unsure"]
      }
    },
    "required": ["category"]
  },
  "stream": false
}
